      ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 \\cdot e^{y} \\ln{(x )}+4 x y^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 \\cdot e^{y} \\ln{(x )})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x y^{3}) \\\\  & =3 \\cdot e^{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln{(x )})+4 y^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =3 \\cdot e^{y}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})+4 y^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{y}}{x}+4 y^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(2,1)} & =\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{(1)}}{(2)}+4 (1)^{3} \\\\  & =4 + \\frac{3 \\cdot e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
