    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=2 \\sin{(x )} \\cos{(y )}-x y,$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(2 \\pi,\\pi).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 \\sin{(x )} \\cos{(y )}-x y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 \\sin{(x )} \\cos{(y )})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x y) \\\\  & =2 \\cos{(y )}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sin{(x )})-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =2 \\cos{(y )}\\cdot (\\cos{(x )})-y\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2 \\cos{(x )} \\cos{(y )}-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2 \\pi,\\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2 \\pi,\\pi) & =2 \\cos{((2 \\pi) )} \\cos{((\\pi) )}-(\\pi) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (1)-(\\pi) \\\\  & =- \\pi - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
